{"question": "A 65-year-old man with a history of hypertension, obesity, and alcoholic cirrhosis is seen in clinic for follow-up. He feels well and currently drinks 5 glasses of wine each night. Medications include atenolol and lisinopril. On physical exam, temperature is 98.1 deg F (36.7 deg C), blood pressure is 151/82 mmHg, pulse is 71/min, and respirations are 14/min. He has spider angiomata on his chest; no asterixis, jaundice, ascites, or peripheral edema is noted. Screening ultrasound reveals a new liver nodule, and follow up CT demonstrates a 2 cm right hepatic lobe lesion with enhancement in the arterial phase. No hypodense attenuation is seen on the venous or delayed phase. What is the next step in management?", "options": {"A": "Proceed with liver biopsy", "B": "Refer for surgical resection", "C": "Refer for radiofrequency ablation", "D": "Treat with sorafenib", "E": "Observe and get follow-up imaging in 3 months"}, "pred_answer": "C", "gold_answer": "A", "question_domains": ["Hepatology", "Gastroenterology", "Oncology", "Internal Medicine", "Addiction Medicine"], "option_domains": ["Hepatology", "Oncology"], "question_analyses": {"Hepatology": "In this clinical scenario, we have a 65-year-old man with a history of alcoholic cirrhosis who presents for follow-up. He has a new liver nodule that has been identified on ultrasound and confirmed by CT imaging. The characteristics of the lesion are particularly important: it is a 2 cm nodule in the right hepatic lobe that shows enhancement in the arterial phase without hypodense attenuation in the venous or delayed phase. ### Interpretation of the Condition:\nGiven the patient's history of alcoholic cirrhosis and the imaging findings, the most likely diagnosis for the liver nodule is hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC). The enhancement in the arterial phase is a classic feature of HCC, especially in the context of cirrhosis. The absence of hypodense attenuation in the venous or delayed phase further supports this dia